{-
   `cStringLength`, and the two things a NUL scan has to get right that a bounded scan does not.

   - **Where the terminator is**, at every alignment the address can have and every length the string
     can have. The loop reads whole aligned vectors, so which lanes of the first one belong to the
     caller's string is a function of the *address* and not only of the count - and the bytes below
     it are somebody else's, whose zeroes are not this string's terminator. Every case below paints
     the whole region zero first and then writes the string into it, so a head mask that cleared the
     wrong lanes answers before the string starts rather than after it.
   - **That the loop never reads a page it was not given.** `cStringLength`'s argument is almost
     always foreign memory - `argv`, the environment, a `getdents64` block - which carries no
     tail-read guarantee (Design-Vector.md §5.3), and the align-down argument in §5.5 is what makes
     the reads safe anyway. `terminatorAtPageEnd` is that argument's test: a live page with nothing
     mapped after it, and a string whose terminator is its very last byte. An unaligned load at the
     string's own address would fault on every one of those cases; this one may not fault on any.
-}

import Test

let letter = 65 :: U8

{-
   Every alignment against every length.

   `at` runs past a vector's width at every level this is built at - sixteen bytes at v2 and
   thirty-two at v3 - so every residue of the address is covered, and `n` crosses several whole
   blocks. The region is zeroed before each case, which is what makes the head mask's job visible:
   with `at` bytes of zero in front of the string, a scan that forgot to clear the lanes below its
   own address answers a negative length or zero rather than `n`.
-}
@test fn lengthAtEveryAlignment() -> {}:
    let span = 512 :: Size
    let buffer = allocateHeap(span)
    let &bad = 0 :: Int
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    while at < 64:
        let &n = 0 :: Size

        while n <= 200:
            setMemory(buffer, 0 :: U8, span)
            setMemory(buffer + at, letter, n)

            if cStringLength(buffer + at) != n then bad = bad + 1
            n = n + 1

        at = at + 1

    freeHeap(buffer)
    checkEqual(bad, 0)

{-
   The case that is certain rather than unlikely - Design-Vector.md §5.3.

   Two pages are mapped and the second is given back, so the byte after the first page's last one is
   in no mapping at all. A string is then placed so that its terminator *is* that last byte, at every
   length from one to two vectors: the align-down loop reads the block the terminator is in and stops
   there, and no load in it crosses the boundary. A loop that read a vector starting at the string's
   own address would run into the hole on every case here.

   The mapping is given back a page at a time, which is what leaves the hole where the test needs it.
-}
@test fn terminatorAtPageEnd() -> {}:
    let page = systemPageBytes()
    let region = mapMemory(page + page)
    requireTrue(!isNull(region))

    unmapMemory(region + page, page)

    let &bad = 0 :: Int
    let &n = 1 :: Size

    while n <= 64:
        let start = region + page - n - 1
        setMemory(start, letter, n)
        store(region + page - 1, 0 :: U8)

        if cStringLength(start) != n then bad = bad + 1
        n = n + 1

    unmapMemory(region, page)
    checkEqual(bad, 0)

-- An empty C string is a terminator and nothing else, which is the one length the sweep above starts
-- at and the one a scan written as a do-while would answer one for.
@test fn emptyCString() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(64)
    setMemory(buffer, 0 :: U8, 64)
    checkEqual(cStringLength(buffer), 0)
    freeHeap(buffer)

{-
   The round trip, both ways.

   `cString` is the copy with the terminator after it, so its own length counts that terminator and
   the scan over its bytes does not - the two numbers differ by exactly one, which is the whole of
   what the conversion is.
-}
@test fn conversions() -> {}:
    let text = "usr/local/share/yana"
    let terminated = cString(text)

    checkEqual(length(terminated), length(text) + 1)
    checkEqual(cStringLength(stringBytes(terminated)), length(text))
    check(stringFromCString(stringBytes(terminated)) == text)

    let empty = cString("")
    checkEqual(length(empty), 1)
    checkEqual(cStringLength(stringBytes(empty)), 0)
    check(stringFromCString(stringBytes(empty)) == "")

-- A string long enough to cross several whole blocks at every level, so that the steady-state loop
-- runs rather than the head answering on its own.
@test fn longCString() -> {}:
    let &text = String.ofCapacity(300)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < 300:
        String.pushUnit(text, 97 + (truncate(i % 26) :: Int))
        i = i + 1

    let terminated = cString(text)
    checkEqual(cStringLength(stringBytes(terminated)), 300)
    check(stringFromCString(stringBytes(terminated)) == text)

{-
   The path lens, at the two lengths its buffer has an edge at.

   `withPathCString` has no branch in it any more - the buffer holds `maxPathBytes` and a longer path
   is `pathTooLong`'s answer rather than a second arm - so what is left to pin is the boundary
   itself, and it is a boundary in *two* numbers that have to agree: the `4096` the refined array is
   declared with, and `maxPathBytes`, which the const-generic hole keeps from being one number
   (Implementation-Const-Generics.md §10.8). A path of `maxPathBytes - 1` units is the longest that
   fits, and it is exactly the case that writes the terminator into the array's last slot - so a
   buffer declared smaller than `maxPathBytes` overruns its own frame here and nowhere else.
-}
fn repeated(count: Size) -> String:
    let &text = String.ofCapacity(count)
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        String.pushUnit(text, 97 + (truncate(i % 26) :: Int))
        i = i + 1

    return text

fn pathRoundTrip(path: String) -> Bool:
    let p = withPathCString(path)
    return cStringLength(p) == length(path) && stringFromCString(p) == path

@test fn pathBuffer() -> {}:
    check(pathRoundTrip(""))
    check(pathRoundTrip("/tmp/yana"))
    check(pathRoundTrip(repeated(maxPathBytes - 2)))
    check(pathRoundTrip(repeated(maxPathBytes - 1)))

-- The bound itself, which is what every caller in `File` asks before it asks for a copy. `+ 1` for
-- the terminator: a path of `maxPathBytes - 1` units fits and one of `maxPathBytes` does not, and
-- the two cases either side of that are the whole of the contract.
@test fn pathLengthBound() -> {}:
    check(!pathTooLong(""))
    check(!pathTooLong(repeated(maxPathBytes - 1)))
    check(pathTooLong(repeated(maxPathBytes)))
    check(pathTooLong(repeated(maxPathBytes + 1)))

{-
   Two lens calls in one block, which is what `moveFile` writes: the second is a statement of the
   first one's continuation, so both frames are live and both buffers reach the same expression.

   Two `maxPathBytes` buffers in one frame is also what that costs, which is worth having a case
   about: they are `alloca`s and not fills, so nesting is a stack offset rather than eight thousand
   bytes of `rep stos`.
-}
@test fn twoPathsAtOnce() -> {}:
    let from = repeated(40)
    let to = repeated(3000)

    let source = withPathCString(from)
    let destination = withPathCString(to)

    checkEqual(cStringLength(source), length(from))
    checkEqual(cStringLength(destination), length(to))
    check(stringFromCString(source) == from)
    check(stringFromCString(destination) == to)

{-
   The miscompile the first version of this lens was written around, as a case rather than a comment.

   A reference chosen by a branch is rooted in whichever arm produced it, and `extendBorrowUses` in
   `analyze_effects.cpp` lost that root at the merge: the walk followed a `return`-marked call's
   result nowhere, and a phi ended it. The copy's `freeHeap` was then placed before the block that
   read through the pointer, so what came back was the free list's next pointer written over the
   first eight bytes - `cStringLength` of it answered 6 rather than the string's length.

   Both arms are written here because only one of them is the interesting one and neither is
   interesting on its own: `held` owns a heap copy on the `spilled` arm and nothing on the other, and
   it is the *owned* one whose drop moved. `values(buffer)` on the first arm is what makes the merge
   a merge; a phi with an owned arm and a borrowed one is exactly the shape the fix has to tell apart
   from a phi of two owned closures, which drops at its edges and must go on doing so.
-}
lens fn chosenReference(text: String, inline: Bool) -> %U8:
    let &buffer = [] :: @inline(64) @capacity(64) [CodeUnit]
    let spilled = cString(text)

    let base = bitcast(values(buffer)) :: %U8

    if inline:
        copyMemory(base, stringBytes(text), length(text))
        store(base + length(text), 0 :: U8)

    yield if inline then base else stringBytes(spilled)

@test fn rootSurvivesAMerge() -> {}:
    let text = "a-path-of-some-length"

    let fromBuffer = chosenReference(text, True)
    checkEqual(cStringLength(fromBuffer), length(text))
    check(stringFromCString(fromBuffer) == text)

    let fromHeap = chosenReference(text, False)
    checkEqual(cStringLength(fromHeap), length(text))
    check(stringFromCString(fromHeap) == text)
